  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「セイヨウハシバミ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%BB%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A8%E3%82%A6%E3%83%8F%E3%82%B7%E3%83%90%E3%83%9F",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111315432,
      "text": "セイヨウハシバミ は、カバノキ科の落葉低木。種子はヘーゼルナッツ（Hazelnut）と呼ばれて食用となり、クッキーやケーキなどの材料としてよく使われる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Corylus avellana」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corylus_avellana",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1341513740,
      "text": "Corylus avellana, the common hazel, is a species of flowering plant in the birch family Betulaceae. The shrubs usually grow 3–8 metres tall. The nut is round, in contrast to the longer filbert nut. Common hazel is native to Europe and Western Asia."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Corylus avellana」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corylus_avellana#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1341513740,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "There are many cultivars of the hazel, up to 400 cultivars (in 2011) of C. avellana have been named. The list of cultivars includes Barcelona, Butler, Casina, Clark Cosford, Daviana, Delle Langhe, England, Ennis, Fillbert, Halls Giant, Jemtegaard, Kent Cob, Lewis, Tokolyi, Tonda Gentile, Tonda di Giffoni, Tonda Romana, Wanliss Pride, and Willamette. It also includes Polish hazelnuts cultivars: Kataloński and Webba Cenny.\nSome of these are grown for specific qualities of the nut including large nut size, and early and late fruiting cultivars, whereas other are grown as pollinators. The majority of commercial hazelnuts are propagated from root sprouts. Some cultivars are of hybrid origin between common hazel and filbert.\nThe following ornamental cultivars have gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit:\n\n'Contorta' (corkscrew hazel, Harry Lauder's walking stick)\nThis variety has stems that grow in curves and spirals unlike other hazel varieties and puckered leaves. Its long elegant catkins are normal. It was discovered in a hedge in 1863 in Gloucestershire on the Frocester Court Estate and was popularised by owners of local estates such as Henry Reynolds-Moreton, 3rd Earl of Ducie and the horticulturalist Edward Augustus Bowles. It is now popular in UK gardens. \n\n'Red Majestic'\nThis red-purple leaved variety was bred from 'Contorta' so also has a sprawling, contorted habit.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
